@h1 External data — the DataLoader seam h1@

@@@code @load code@@@ is the @strong one strong@ seam between a Wit document and
the outside world. Whether the data comes from a CLI subprocess or an in-process
object, the rest of the pipeline never knows the difference — by the time
@code resolve code@ runs, loaded data is indistinguishable from a hand-written
@@@code #name: code@@@.

@h2 Concept h2@

@@@code @load <alias> load@ code@@@ names data by alias only; a host supplies the
value. @code loadExternalData(doc, source) code@ runs @strong after parse,
before resolve strong@ and rewrites every @@@code @load code@@@ into an ordinary
@code DataDef code@.

@h2 Signatures h2@

@ul
@li @code loadExternalData(doc: Document, source: DataSource) → Document code@. li@
@li @@@code type DataSource = DataLoader | Record<string, unknown> code@@@ — a
loader function @strong or strong@ a plain dict @@@code { alias: value } code@@@
(the simplest embedded case). li@
@li @code type DataLoader = (req: DataLoadRequest) => unknown code@. li@
@li @@@code interface DataLoadRequest = { alias: string; args: Record<string, string>; loc: Loc } code@@@.
@code args code@ are the named captures —
@@@code @load cite |key devlin2019| load@ code@@@ gives
@@@code { key: 'devlin2019' } code@@@. @@@code |as name| code@@@ rebinds the
resulting @code DataDef code@ name; @@@code |from alias| code@@@ supplies the
alias when there is no body prose. li@
@li @code toDataValue(value: unknown, loc: Loc) → DataValue code@ — normalises
plain JSON into the Wit value model. It is the @strong only strong@ shape that
crosses the seam. li@
ul@

@h2 Basic — the dict form h2@

The simplest source is a plain object keyed by alias:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';
import { loadExternalData, resolve, expand } from '@witlang/runtime';

const doc = loadExternalData(
  parse('@load meta load@\n\n@meta.version', 'report.wit'),
  { meta: { version: '0.4.0' } });

// the first child is now dataDef{ name: 'meta', value: record{ version: '0.4.0' } }
renderHtml(expand(resolve(doc)));   // → <article class="wit-doc"><p>0.4.0</p></article>
pre@@@

@h2 Realistic — the function form h2@

For dynamic data, pass a loader. It is called once per @@@code @load code@@@,
lazily, per alias:

@@@pre
const loaded = loadExternalData(doc, (req) => {
  // req = { alias, args: { …captures }, loc }
  return fetchSync(req.alias, req.args);   // your synchronous I/O
});
pre@@@

@aside(class note) ⚠️ The loader is called @strong synchronously. strong@ There
is no @code await code@ in the pipeline. If your data source is async, fetch it
into a map first and have the loader return from that map. aside@

@h2 Edge — toDataValue h2@

@code toDataValue code@ is how plain JSON becomes a @code DataValue code@: an
object becomes an ordered @code Record code@, an array a @code Collection code@,
primitives become scalars, and @code null code@ or @code undefined code@ become
@code NullValue code@.

@@@pre
import { toDataValue } from '@witlang/runtime';

toDataValue({ a: 1, b: [2, 3] }, loc);
// → record{ fields: [ { a: numberValue 1 },
//                      { b: collection[ numberValue 2, numberValue 3 ] } ] }
pre@@@

@h2 Every option / edge h2@

@ul
@li @strong Dict source, missing alias. strong@ A dict with no entry for an alias
throws @code no data provided for alias "x" code@, which surfaces as
@code E_LOAD_FAILED code@ at the load site. li@
@li @strong Throwing loader. strong@ Any error your loader throws becomes
@code E_LOAD_FAILED code@, carrying the message and the load @code loc code@. li@
@li @strong Downstream, loaded data is ordinary data. strong@ It flows through
@@@code @meta.version code@@@, @@@code (each @team as p) code@@@,
@@@code @table |rows @downloads| code@@@, and interpolation with no special
casing. li@
@li @strong Raw load is skipped. strong@ A @@@code @@load code@@@ raw node is not
treated as a load (the guard checks @code raw !== true code@). li@
@li @strong The CLI is one implementation of this seam. strong@ Its
@code makeDataLoader code@ reads @code wit.sources.json code@, runs the aliased
program (gated by @code --allow-exec code@), and parses stdout by declared
format; @code env code@ is a built-in source returning the process environment.
See @a |href /docs/build/cli-reference/| CLI reference a@. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting @code loadExternalData code@ to @em fetch. em@ It only calls your
loader or reads your dict — you own the I/O. li@
@li Returning a class instance instead of plain JSON. Only object, array, and
scalar values cross via @code toDataValue code@. li@
@li Forgetting it must run @strong before strong@ @code resolve code@. Order is
parse → load → resolve → expand → render. li@
ul@

